"Summons—Enforcement of State Housing Law | California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/jcc-form/SUM-145)\nciteturn1642search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; # Summons—Enforcement of State Housing Law (SUM-145)Tells the owner of a building that a court case has started because the owner did not fix a problem with the building after getting a notice saying the problem must be fixed and what can happen if a response is not filed in 10 days.\n\n# Summons—Enforcement of State Housing Law (SUM-145)\n\nTells the owner of a building that a court case has started because the owner did not fix a problem with the building after getting a notice saying the problem must be fixed and what can happen if a response is not filed in 10 days.\n\nGet form SUM-145\n\nEffective: July 1, 2009--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n` (https://courts.ca.gov/sites/default/files/courts/default/2024-10/min102408.pdf)\nciteturn1642search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 14.5 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago; The Judicial Council, effective January 1, 2009, adopted mandatory form *Summons—Enforcement of State Housing Law* (form SUM-145).Item A14  Trial Courts: Release of Court Records (amend Cal.\nCouncil action  \nThe Judicial Council, effective January 1, 2009, adopted mandatory form *Summons—Enforcement of State Housing Law* (form SUM-145).\n\nItem A14  Trial Courts: Release of Court Records (amend Cal. Rules of Court, rule 2.400)\n\nThe Civil and Small Claims Advisory Committee recommended that rule 2.400 of the California Rules of Court on court records be amended to provide that filed papers may be released to an attorney of record within the court facility. The rule currently provides that papers filed in a court may be released only to a “court officer or authorized court personnel for use in a court facility.” The term “court officer” is ambiguous; it is unclear whether it applies to attorneys or court administrative officers or both. The amendment will clarify that papers may be released to “an attorney of record.”\n\nCouncil action  \nThe Judicial Council amended California Rules of Court, rule 2.400 to clarify that filed papers may be released to an attorney of record within the court facility.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSUM-145\nSUMMONS  | SUM-145\n--- | ---\nSUMMONS\n(CITA (https://courts.ca.gov/documents/sum145.pdf)\nciteturn1642search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.7 years ago; SUM-145 ... Online Self-Help Center (www.courtinfo.ca.gov/selfhelp), your county law library, or the courthouse nearest you. ... If you do not file your response on time, you may lose the case by default, and your wages, money, and property\nSUM-145\nSUMMONS  | SUM-145\n--- | ---\nSUMMONS\n(CITACION JUDICIAL)\nENFORCEMENT OF STATE HOUSING LAW\n(CUMPLIMIENTO DE LA LEY DE VIVIENDA ESTATAL)\nNOTICE TO DEFENDANT:\n(AVISO AL DEMANDADO):\nYOU ARE BEING SUED BY PLAINTIFF:\n(LO ESTÁ DEMANDANDO EL DEMANDANTE):  | FOR COURT USE ONLY\nTo k(SeOeLOp P AoRtAh UeSrO pDEe LoAp ClOeR TfEr)om\nseeing what you entered on\nyour form, please press the\nClear This Form button at the\nend of the form when finished.\nYou have 10 CALENDAR DAYS after this summons and legal papers are served on you to file a written response at this court and have a copy\nserved on the plaintiff. A letter or phone call will not protect you. Your written response must be in proper legal form if you want the court to hear your\ncase. There may be a court form that you can use for your response. You can find these court forms and more information at the California Courts\nOnline Self-Help Center (www.courtinfo.ca.gov/selfhelp), your county law library, or the courthouse nearest you. If you cannot pay the filing fee, ask\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n| California Courts | Self Help Guide (https://selfhelp.courts.ca.gov/find-forms/all-by-c